proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "button_center_xy",
      "description": "After the final placement and withdrawal, the button can be occluded even though it was visible earlier. The existing count and milestone correctly select button approach, but do not supply its destination; the policy guessed a different location. This breaks SUFFICIENCY. Add the most recently reliably localized world-frame center [x,y] of the stationary gray button press surface. Initialize to null until it can be localized from an available calibrated image. Update when a visible button permits reliable localization, accounting for the press surface's elevation above the table; otherwise retain the previous value, including through occlusion and cube manipulation. Annotate using evidence available by that cycle, not later views. Example: [-0.1937, -0.1556].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "button_center_xy",
      "description": "After the final cube withdrawal, correct placement count and milestone can select button approach while the button itself is hidden. Without its earlier location, the destination is not recoverable, breaking SUFFICIENCY. Initialize this field to null. Whenever the current cycle's calibrated images clearly expose the button and permit reliable localization, record the world-frame [x,y] center of its gray press surface, accounting for its elevation rather than projecting the cap pixel onto the table. Retain the incoming value when the button is occluded or localization is unreliable; do not replace it with the TCP position or a guess. Example: [-0.1937, -0.1556]."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "The failures occurred during target descent, repeated pickup alignment, and button approach or pressing. The placement counts and achieved milestones were correct, but the policy either guessed an occluded button location, projected elevated features onto the wrong plane, or copied the achieved TCP x,y despite tracking error. These are SUFFICIENCY failures, not reasons to change the count or milestone transitions.\n\nStrengthen the localization rules for all these actions. For a calibrated image pixel p, form the world ray with origin C=-R^T t and direction d=R^T K^-1 p, and intersect it with the physical plane of the feature being localized. Use the table plane for the target ring. For a resting cube, use the center of its visible top face and its top-face plane, approximately z=0.04 m; its recovered x,y is the pickup center, not the table-plane intersection of that pixel. Do not use the resting-cube top-plane height for a held cube. Localize the gray button press-surface center with its elevation accounted for using the button geometry and calibrated views, rather than treating its cap pixel as a table-plane point or substituting the visible white base's image center. Prefer a clear current view; when the button is occluded or the current estimate is unreliable, use button_center_xy. Never guess another object's location as the button destination.\n\nExplicitly distinguish recognition of a completed motion from precise alignment. Achieving target_hover or button_hover allows the next manipulation step, but does not establish that the actual TCP x,y equals the intended object center. At target_hover, re-estimate the ring center and command descent at that center to z=0.005 with opening, correcting lateral tracking error during descent rather than copying the achieved TCP x,y. At release_retracted with placements remaining, perform the dedicated open-hand alignment at z=0.15 over the height-corrected current cube center.\n\nAfter the final withdrawal, approach the currently localized or retained button center at z=0.15 with open fingers and orientation [-3.1416,0,0]. At button_hover, command closure at the button center x,y and nominal z=0.15, correcting any hover tracking offset. At button_closed, descend at that same independently localized center to z=0.0075 with closed fingers; do not use the achieved hover TCP x,y as the button location. Use the button center for retraction as well. Apply the existing 2 mm minimum-waypoint-displacement safeguard only after constructing the center-corrected waypoint: if its lateral or vertical correction already supplies sufficient displacement, do not add an unnecessary upward offset. Preserve the existing contact-depth tolerance, placement counting, milestone transitions, and final-withdrawal requirement."
}

